# Site Chat — messaging, file sharing & calls

Real-time chat you can drop into your existing site: text messages, images,
videos, audio, documents, voice notes, and one-to-one voice/video calls.

## Run it standalone (fastest way to try it)

```bash
npm install
npm start
```

Open `http://localhost:3000` in two different browser windows (or two
devices on the same network), enter two different names, and chat between
them.

## How it works

- **server.js** — Express serves the `public/` folder, Socket.io handles
  real-time messaging and call signaling, and `multer` handles file uploads
  (saved to `public/uploads/`).
- **public/index.html / style.css / client.js** — the chat UI: message
  list, file/voice-note attach buttons, and the call overlay. All calling
  logic uses the browser's built-in WebRTC — media streams go directly
  between the two browsers, the server only relays the initial connection
  info (signaling), so it stays fast and cheap to run.

## Merging into your existing server.js

If you already have a `server.js` running your site, don't run two
servers — merge this one in instead:

1. Copy `public/index.html`, `public/style.css`, `public/client.js` into
   your existing `public/` (or static) folder — put them in a `/chat`
   subfolder if you don't want them to replace your homepage, e.g.
   `public/chat/index.html`, and link to `/chat` from your site.
2. In your existing server.js, add:
   ```js
   const http = require("http");
   const { Server } = require("socket.io");
   const server = http.createServer(app); // `app` = your existing Express app
   const io = new Server(server);
   // ...paste the upload route and io.on("connection", ...) block from
   // this project's server.js...
   server.listen(PORT, ...); // instead of app.listen(...)
   ```
3. Add the two dependencies to your `package.json`: `socket.io`, `multer`,
   `nanoid`.

## Notes & limits (read before you rely on this in production)

- **No accounts yet.** Anyone who types a name can join — there's no
  password or identity check. If your site already has logins, replace
  the "join" step with your real user ID/name instead of a free-text box.
- **No chat history.** Messages live only in the browser tab; nothing is
  saved to a database. Add one (e.g. MongoDB/Postgres) if you need people
  to see old messages after refreshing.
- **Calls are one-to-one**, not group calls.
- **HTTPS is required for calling/mic/camera to work** once this is live
  on a real domain (browsers block camera/mic access on plain HTTP,
  `localhost` is exempted for testing).
- File uploads are capped at 100MB and stored directly on disk — fine for
  testing, but for production you'll likely want cloud storage (S3, etc.)
  and virus scanning if strangers can upload.